page cmd_destroy {
  title = "vmlab destroy"
  chapter_header "vmlab destroy" {
    kicker       = "Commands · reference"
    reading_time = "3 min read"
    updated      = "2026-10-29"
    version      = "vmlab 0.35"
  }

  p <<DOC
    `vmlab destroy` stops the lab in the current directory and deletes
    everything it materialised: the clones, the container overlays and named
    volumes, the lab-local state under `.vmlab/`, and the dynamic network
    configuration. The lab file is untouched. The next
    [vmlab up](cmd_up) starts from fresh clones and runs first-boot
    provisioning again.
  DOC

  code sh { source = "vmlab destroy [--discard-guest-changes]" }

  table {
    rows:
      | "Option" | "Meaning" |
      | "`--discard-guest-changes`" | "Destroy even where a dev machine's workspace holds guest work the host has not taken, and lose that work." |
      | "`-h`, `--help`" | "Print help." |
  }

  h2 "What it does"

  p "The verb works in this order:"

  list {
    style = :numbered
    li "With a lab daemon running, it asks the daemon to destroy the lab. The daemon first asks every dev machine's workspace whether the guest holds work the host has not taken, and refuses if one does. It then stops every workspace syncer, force-stops every machine, waits up to 30 seconds for each to settle, and removes `.vmlab/` and each machine's runtime directory."
    li "With no daemon running, it reads the same answer off the sync ledgers in `.vmlab/`, and then removes `.vmlab/` itself if the directory exists."
    li "It asks the supervisor to release the lab, which reaps the lab daemon."
  }

  p "The last line printed is `lab \"<name>\" destroyed`."

  p <<DOC
    Everything a machine had that was not in its template goes: [snapshots](guide_snapshots)
    live inside the clones, so they go with them, and a machine marked
    diverged by [vmlab machine repair-agent](cmd_machine) comes back on its
    template's sealed agent, unless the next [vmlab up](cmd_up) refreshes it
    again; `agent_update = false` stops that. The workspace sync ledger in
    `.vmlab/` goes too.
  DOC

  callout "The workspace survives, the guest tree does not" {
    kind = :warning
    body = "A dev machine's source lives in the host workspace directory, which `destroy` never touches. The guest copy is deleted with the clone."
  }

  h2 "Guest work the host has not taken"

  p <<DOC
    Deleting the guest copy loses nothing only while the two copies are in
    step, so `destroy` asks first. A running [workspace](guide_dev) gets a
    last sync pass, which carries what it can. A stopped one answers from its
    sync ledger: a halt its last pass recorded, what the last pass before
    `vmlab down` could not carry, or the fact that a `down --force` took no
    last pass. Where guest work is left, or could be, `destroy` refuses
    before it stops anything, names the machine and the paths, and changes
    nothing. One refusal stops the destroy of the whole lab.
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    To keep the work, bring the machine up so the workspace syncs, or answer
    the halt with `vmlab dev sync resolve`. To lose it, pass
    `--discard-guest-changes`. A workspace that owes a re-seed after a
    snapshot restore is not refused: its guest tree is rewritten from the
    host anyway.
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    Templates in the store are never written to by a lab and are not removed
    here; use [vmlab template](cmd_template) for that. To destroy a lab by
    name from any directory use [vmlab lab destroy](cmd_lab).
  DOC

  h2 "Worktree labs"

  p <<DOC
    In the main checkout of a [worktree lab](guide_worktrees) family,
    `destroy` refuses while any linked worktree's disk backs onto the
    freeze, with or without a daemon running, and changes nothing. The
    refusal names each worktree and the generations it uses, and says to
    run `vmlab destroy` in each to release it. With no worktree using the
    freeze, `destroy` deletes `.vmlab/freeze/` and `worktree.json` with the
    rest of `.vmlab/`, so the frozen state is gone for good.
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    In a worktree lab, `destroy` deletes only the worktree's own overlays and
    copies and never touches the frozen files. It is how a worktree releases
    the freeze, and the next `up` there clones from the newest generation.
    It also releases the worktree's port slot and the ports recorded for it
    in the main checkout's `worktree-ports.json`, with or without a daemon
    running, so the next worktree lab can take them.
  DOC

  h2 "Examples"

  p "Tear a lab down and rebuild it from scratch:"
  code sh { source = <<'SRC'
vmlab destroy
vmlab up
SRC
  }

  h2 "Exit status"

  p <<DOC
    Exit status is 0 when the lab's state was removed, whether or not a
    daemon was running. A lab directory that cannot be found, a machine that
    cannot be stopped, a `.vmlab/` directory that cannot be removed, or a
    workspace holding guest work without `--discard-guest-changes` exits
    1 (`failed`). A main lab whose freeze a worktree lab still uses exits 5
    (`conflict`). A usage error exits 2.
  DOC
}
